"cites":0,"kind":null}],"trials":[],"label":null,"reddit":[],"forums":[],"news":[],"social":[],"web":[{"title":"Gyaru (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gyaru"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Style & presentation","chain":["Rhinestones and charms applied to nails and accessories","Matches gyaru makeup and hair","Cohesive decorated look"],"effect":"direct","text":"Rhinestone and charm decoration on nails and accessories adds the glam finish of the gyaru look."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":null,"lead":["Decoden, also called keitai art, is a gyaru-associated hobby that originated in Japan. It involves decorating phones and devices with acrylic, rhinestones, crystals, silicone and polymer clay.[1]","Older decorations were glued directly onto phones, while newer ones are usually applied to smartphone cases.[1] Gyaru nails are typically long, artificial and heavily decorated, with 3D charms among the elements, so the style carries over to personal grooming.[1]","Gyaru itself is known for tanned skin, voluminous dyed hair, dramatic eye makeup and large lashes.[1] No studies have examined decoden."],"infobox":[["Origin","Japan[1]"],["Materials","Acrylic, rhinestones, crystals, silicone, polymer clay[1]"],["Associated style","Gyaru[1]"]],"sections":[{"h":"Description","paras":["Decoden covers items with decorative pieces; keitai refers to Japanese mobile phones. The practice is paired with collecting phone charms and taking purikura photo-sticker pictures.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Contests and media","paras":["In 2009 Acer held a decoden contest for its Aspire One netbook.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[{"year":"2009","event":"Acer runs a decoden contest for the Aspire One.[1]"}],"refs":[{"title":"Gyaru","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gyaru","source":"Wikipedia","year":null,"type":"encyclopedia"}],"studies":[],"reddit":[],"forums":[]}